--TEST--
An `#[Access]` whose `allow` is text rather than a name is refused, because what the compiler checks is that the name resolves — `rule:attributes/access-payload`, `rule:security/access-is-checked-for-presence-not-meaning`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Audience
//
// `Core` names one audience and the application names every other one, and
// neither of them is a string. A decision spelled `"public"` resolves to
// nothing, so nothing can check that whoever declared this route and whoever
// dispatches to it mean the same thing by the word. A misspelt string would
// open a route quietly, and a misspelt name does not compile.
class Pricing {
    #[Core\Route(path: "/pricing", method: Core\Http\Method::Get, name: "Pricing::show")]
    #[Core\Access(allow: "public")]
    public function show(): string { return "p"; }
}

// What the author meant, spelled the one way that carries it. It sits here so
// the refusal above is a fact about the text and not about this route, this
// path or this attribute.
class Help {
    #[Core\Route(path: "/help", method: Core\Http\Method::Get, name: "Help::show")]
    #[Core\Access(allow: Core\Audience::Public)]
    public function show(): string { return "h"; }
}

echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0761]: an access decision is a name rather than a value
   --> case.nvs:11:26
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
